UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 12, Lesson 1: Voice and body in drama', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"In drama, voice and body work together to show a character. Voice has volume (loud or soft), pace (fast or slow), pitch (high or low) and tone (the feeling in the voice, such as friendly or angry). Body has posture (how you stand), gesture (hand and arm movements) and facial expression. Change these on purpose. A scared character might speak quickly and softly while a giant might speak slowly and loudly. Use a clear voice so the audience can hear, but never shout or strain your throat.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nSay ''Come here, please.'' in three ways: as a worried parent, an excited friend and a sleepy grandfather. Describe the pace, volume and body for each.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: worried parent is fast and quiet with a frown, excited friend is fast and loud with a big gesture and a smile, sleepy grandfather is slow and soft with a yawn. The same words change meaning when voice and body change.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking a loud voice is always a good voice. A good drama voice is clear and matched to the character, and sometimes a whisper is more powerful than a shout.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nChoose a sentence of your own and write four ways to say it with different volume, pace, pitch and body choices. Perform two of them for someone and ask them to guess the character or feeling.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"In drama, voice and body work together to show a character. Voice has volume (loud or soft), pace (fast or slow), pitch (high or low) and tone (the feeling in the voice, such as friendly or angry). Body has posture (how you stand), gesture (hand and arm movements) and facial expression. Change these on purpose. A scared character might speak quickly and softly while a giant might speak slowly and loudly. Use a clear voice so the audience can hear, but never shout or strain your throat.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-DRA-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Choose a sentence of your own and write four ways to say it with different volume, pace, pitch and body choices. Perform two of them for someone and ask them to guess the character or feeling. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: worried parent is fast and quiet with a frown, excited friend is fast and loud with a big gesture and a smile, sleepy grandfather is slow and soft with a yawn. The same words change… Reasoning: I used volume by attending to pace rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking a loud voice is always a good voice. A good drama voice is clear and matched to the character, and sometimes a whisper is more powerful than a shout. Correction: Strong answer: worried parent is fast and quiet with a frown, excited friend is fast and loud with a big gesture and a smile, sleepy grandfather is slow and… Why: the correction restores accurate use of volume.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking a loud voice is always a good voice. A good drama voice is clear and matched to the character, and sometimes a whisper is more powerful than a shout. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating volume on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with volume, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answer: worried parent is fast and quiet with a frown, excited friend is fast and loud with a big gesture and a smile, sleepy grandfather is slow and… This matches the lesson skill for volume.","options":["Strong answer: worried parent is fast and quiet with a frown, excited friend is fast and loud with a big gesture and a smile, sleepy grandfather is slow and…","Thinking a loud voice is always a good voice. A good drama voice is clear and matched to the character, and sometimes a whisper is more powerful than a shout.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work."],"question":"Say ''Come here, please.'' in three ways: as a worried parent, an excited friend and a sleepy grandfather. Describe the pace, volume and body for each. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Thinking a loud voice is always a good voice. A good drama voice is clear and matched to the character, and sometimes a whisper is more powerful than a shout. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Careful use of pace with clear evidence.","Thinking a loud voice is always a good voice. A good drama voice is clear and matched to the character, and sometimes a whisper is more powerful than a shout.","In drama, voice and body work together to show a character. Voice has volume (loud or soft), pace (fast or slow), pitch (high or low) and tone (the feeling in…","Strong answer: worried parent is fast and quiet with a frown, excited friend is fast and loud with a big gesture and a smile, sleepy grandfather is slow and…"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising volume?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named pace, not by copying the model.","options":["Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the pace used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right."],"question":"A learner is practising volume. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answer: worried parent is fast and quiet with a frown, excited friend is fast and loud with a big gesture and a smile, sleepy grandfather is slow and… uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Thinking a loud voice is always a good voice. A good drama voice is clear and matched to the character, and sometimes a whisper is more powerful than a shout.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: worried parent is fast and quiet with a frown, excited friend is fast and loud with a big gesture and a smile, sleepy grandfather is slow and…"],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about volume?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Thinking a loud voice is always a good voice. A good drama voice is clear and matched to the character, and sometimes a whisper is more powerful than a shout.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about volume.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about pace?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Thinking a loud voice is always a good voice. A good drama voice is clear and matched to the character, and sometimes a whisper is more powerful than a shout.","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Example: for a giant, Omar speaks slowly in a deep, loud voice and takes big stomping steps with his arms wide. For a mouse, he speaks…"],"question":"Using this lesson idea — In drama, voice and body work together to show a character. Voice has volume (loud or soft), pace (fast or… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Thinking a loud voice is always a good voice. A good drama voice is clear and matched to the character, and sometimes a whisper is more powerful than a shout.","Choose a sentence of your own and write four ways to say it with different volume, pace, pitch and body choices. Perform two of them for someone and…"],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of volume?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating volume on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: worried parent is fast and quiet with a frown, excited friend is fast and loud with a big gesture and a smile, sleepy grandfather is slow and soft with a yawn. The same words change… Reasoning: I used volume by attending to pace rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with volume, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking a loud voice is always a good voice. A good drama voice is clear and matched to the character, and sometimes a whisper is more powerful than a shout. Correction: Strong answer: worried parent is fast and quiet with a frown, excited friend is fast and loud with a big gesture and a smile, sleepy grandfather is slow and… Why: the correction restores accurate use of volume.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w12-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise volume.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Drama","success_criteria":["I can use volume with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify pace and explain why it matters.","I can practise gesture independently on a new prompt.","I can notice facial expression needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"In drama, voice and body work together to show a character. Voice has volume (loud or soft), pace (fast or slow), pitch (high or low) and tone (the feeling in the voice, such as friendly or angry). Body has posture (how you stand), gesture (hand and arm movements) and facial…","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","In drama, voice and body work together to show a character. Voice has volume (loud or soft), pace (fast or slow), pitch (high or low) and tone (the feeling in the voice, such as friendly or angry). Body has posture (how you stand), gesture (hand and arm movements) and facial expression. Change these on purpose. A scared character might speak quickly and softly while a giant might speak slowly and loudly. Use a clear voice so the audience can hear, but never shout or strain your throat.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about volume?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: for a giant, Omar speaks slowly in a deep, loud voice and takes big stomping steps with his arms wide. For a mouse, he speaks quickly in a high, soft voice with tiny steps and his hands held close. The changes in volume, pace, pitch and posture make two clearly different characters.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSay ''Come here, please.'' in three ways: as a worried parent, an excited friend and a sleepy grandfather. Describe the pace, volume and body for each.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: worried parent is fast and quiet with a frown, excited friend is fast and loud with a big gesture and a smile, sleepy grandfather is slow and soft with a yawn. The same words change meaning when voice and body change.","explain":"In drama, voice and body work together to show a character. Voice has volume (loud or soft), pace (fast or slow), pitch (high or low) and tone (the feeling in the voice, such as friendly or angry). Body has posture (how you stand), gesture (hand and arm movements) and facial expression. Change these on purpose. A scared character might speak quickly and softly while a giant might speak slowly and loudly. Use a clear voice so the audience can hear, but never shout or strain your throat.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking a loud voice is always a good voice. A good drama voice is clear and matched to the character, and sometimes a whisper is more powerful than a shout. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Voice choices for two characters.","caption":"Voice shows character.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Giant</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">slow, loud, deep</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Mouse</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">quick, soft, high</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: for a giant, Omar speaks slowly in a deep, loud voice and takes big stomping steps with his arms wide. For a mo Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: for a giant, Omar speaks slowly in a deep, loud voice and takes big stomping steps with his arms wide. For a mo Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about volume?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: for a giant, Omar speaks slowly in a deep, loud voice and takes big stomping steps with his arms wide. For a mouse, he speaks quickly in a high, soft voice with tiny steps and his hands held close. The changes in volume, pace, pitch and posture make two clearly different characters. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Body choices for two characters.","caption":"Body shows character.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Posture</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">big and wide</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Gesture</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">small and close</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about volume?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking a loud voice is always a good voice. A good drama voice is clear and matched to the character, and sometimes a whisper is more powerful than a shout. Here is a more helpful example: Example: for a giant, Omar speaks slowly in a deep, loud voice and takes big stomping steps with his arms wide. For a mouse, he speaks quickly in a high, soft voice with tiny steps and his hands held close. The changes in volume, pace, pitch and posture make two clearly different characters.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: worried parent is fast and quiet with a frown, excited friend is fast and loud with a big gesture and a smile, sleepy grandfather is slow and soft with a yawn. The same words change meaning when voice and body change.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Thinking a loud voice is always a good voice. A good drama voice is clear and matched to the character, and sometimes a whisper is more powerful than a shout. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about volume?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nIn drama, voice and body work together to show a character. Voice has volume (loud or soft), pace (fast or slow), pitch (high or low) and tone (the feeling in the voice, such as friendly or angry). Body has posture (how you stand), gesture (hand and arm movements) and facial expression. Change these on purpose. A scared character might speak quickly and softly while a giant might speak slowly and loudly. Use a clear voice so the audience can hear, but never shout or strain your throat. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 12, Lesson 1: Voice and body in drama","unit_number":3,"unit_title":"Role, character and storytelling","week":12,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: for a giant, Omar speaks slowly in a deep, loud voice and takes big stomping steps with his arms wide. For a mouse, he speaks quickly in a high, soft voice with tiny steps and his hands held close. The changes in volume, pace, pitch and posture make two clearly different characters.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Voice choices for two characters.","caption":"Voice shows character.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Giant</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">slow, loud, deep</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Mouse</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">quick, soft, high</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Body choices for two characters.","caption":"Body shows character.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Posture</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">big and wide</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Gesture</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">small and close</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y3-w12-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
